    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Could you go into a bit more detail on the specific chemical process of how that pH change affects solubility? Like, how does the concentration of hydrogen ions actually interact with the iron and manganese ions in the soil solution?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The solubility of micronutrients like iron ($\\text{Fe}^{3+}$) and manganese ($\\text{Mn}^{2+}$) is highly dependent on hydrogen ion ($\\text{H}^{+}$) concentration, which dictates $\\text{pH}$. As $\\text{pH}$ increases (meaning $\\text{H}^{+}$ concentration decreases), the $\\text{OH}^{-}$ concentration rises, leading to the formation of insoluble hydroxides. For example, at higher $\\text{pH}$, dissolved $\\text{Fe}^{3+}$ readily reacts with hydroxide ions to form highly insoluble iron hydroxides, effectively locking up the nutrient. Conversely, lower $\\text{pH}$ keeps these metals dissolved and biologically available for vine uptake.",
      "source": "target",
